1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,148
Where is the most sacred river of the Rigveda today?

2
00:00:03,148 --> 00:00:07,084
Look from above the desert, and you only see sand.

3
00:00:07,084 --> 00:00:09,445
But in ISRO's satellite images,

4
00:00:09,445 --> 00:00:11,806
lines can be seen beneath the sand.

5
00:00:11,806 --> 00:00:16,135
These lines look like the path of an old river. Come,

6
00:00:16,135 --> 00:00:18,890
let's unfold this story with proof.

7
00:00:18,890 --> 00:00:20,791
Imagine you are up in the sky,

8
00:00:20,791 --> 00:00:24,593
looking from Haryana towards Rajasthan and Gujarat.

9
00:00:24,593 --> 00:00:27,254
Below are fields, villages, and desert.

10
00:00:27,254 --> 00:00:30,296
But the satellite's eye catches something else.

11
00:00:30,296 --> 00:00:34,478
Moisture buried under the ground, and thin strips of old sand,

12
00:00:34,478 --> 00:00:37,900
as if someone drew a river with a pencil.

13
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:42,006
Very old texts say a great river once flowed here.

14
00:00:42,006 --> 00:00:46,112
Its name was Saraswati. The Rigveda calls it sacred and mighty.

15
00:00:46,112 --> 00:00:50,217
And the Mahabharata says that river vanished in the desert.

16
00:00:50,217 --> 00:00:53,950
So is the line seen by the satellite that same lost river?

17
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:57,620
The answer is not that simple. Scientists are divided,

18
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:01,290
and so are historians. Some say yes, this is the Saraswati.

19
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:04,227
Some say the name came from somewhere else.

20
00:01:04,227 --> 00:01:07,163
In this video we will look at the texts, satellite, excavations and debate,

21
00:01:07,163 --> 00:01:11,200
one by one. Every claim will come with its label.

22
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:12,841
First, let's go to the texts,

23
00:01:12,841 --> 00:01:15,139
because the story starts from there.

24
00:01:15,139 --> 00:01:18,421
The Rigveda is counted among the oldest texts in the world.

25
00:01:18,421 --> 00:01:21,047
It has many hymns in praise of rivers.

26
00:01:21,047 --> 00:01:22,688
And it has one hymn

27
00:01:22,688 --> 00:01:25,970
that lists the rivers in a line like a map.

28
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:30,116
This hymn is called the Nadistuti Sukta, meaning praise of the rivers,

29
00:01:30,116 --> 00:01:31,623
a song of their praise.

30
00:01:31,623 --> 00:01:35,392
In it, the rivers are listed from east to west.

31
00:01:35,392 --> 00:01:38,407
First the Ganga, then the Yamuna, then the Saraswati, then the Shutudri,

32
00:01:38,407 --> 00:01:41,422
which we today call the Sutlej. That means the Saraswati

33
00:01:41,422 --> 00:01:44,060
was somewhere between the Yamuna and the Sutlej.

34
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:47,746
This is no small thing. The text is not just naming it,

35
00:01:47,746 --> 00:01:51,097
it is also telling the place. Look at today's map,

36
00:01:51,097 --> 00:01:54,783
and between the Yamuna and the Sutlej lies the area of Haryana.

37
00:01:54,783 --> 00:01:56,124
And that is the very area

38
00:01:56,124 --> 00:01:59,139
where the satellite sees lines of an old river.

39
00:01:59,139 --> 00:02:01,150
This is where the story gets interesting.

40
00:02:01,150 --> 00:02:05,423
Another mantra of the Rigveda describes the Saraswati as a very big river.

41
00:02:05,423 --> 00:02:08,271
He says the Saraswati comes out of the mountains,

42
00:02:08,271 --> 00:02:10,407
and goes straight to the sea.

43
00:02:10,407 --> 00:02:14,680
On the way, it nourishes living beings with its milk-like water.

44
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:18,240
Meaning, this was not a rain-fed drain, it was a full flowing river.

45
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:19,901
Now let's come to the Mahabharata,

46
00:02:19,901 --> 00:02:22,226
which is considered a later text.

47
00:02:22,226 --> 00:02:25,547
In its Shalya Parva, the story of the Saraswati changes.

48
00:02:25,547 --> 00:02:28,204
Here the river reaches the desert and dries up.

49
00:02:28,204 --> 00:02:31,857
It sinks into the ground. The place where it vanished,

50
00:02:31,857 --> 00:02:33,850
that holy site is called Vinasana.

51
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:37,297
Vinasana itself means the place where something is destroyed,

52
00:02:37,297 --> 00:02:38,331
where it disappears.

53
00:02:38,331 --> 00:02:42,123
The text gives the reason for this too, in the form of a story.

54
00:02:42,123 --> 00:02:45,914
So think about it: in the Rigveda, the Saraswati flows from the mountain to the sea.

55
00:02:45,914 --> 00:02:49,361
By the time of the Mahabharata, it gets lost in the desert.

56
00:02:49,361 --> 00:02:50,740
What happened in between?

57
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:53,574
The text has raised the question, now it's science's turn.

58
00:02:53,574 --> 00:02:55,700
If there really was a river here,

59
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:59,596
then its marks must be left in the ground. When a river dries up,

60
00:02:59,596 --> 00:03:02,430
it leaves behind its path. Sand, gravel,

61
00:03:02,430 --> 00:03:03,847
and moisture buried below.

62
00:03:03,847 --> 00:03:07,390
To find these, scientists took help from the sky.

63
00:03:07,390 --> 00:03:10,766
This method is called remote sensing, meaning reading the land

64
00:03:10,766 --> 00:03:13,129
from far away, through the eye of a satellite.

65
00:03:13,129 --> 00:03:15,493
A satellite doesn't just take photos. It also picks up moisture,

66
00:03:15,493 --> 00:03:18,193
soil color and heat.

67
00:03:18,193 --> 00:03:19,882
The old path of a dried river

68
00:03:19,882 --> 00:03:22,920
looks slightly different from the land around it.

69
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:27,422
Scientists at ISRO's Regional Remote Sensing Service Centre did exactly this.

70
00:03:27,422 --> 00:03:31,925
They took data from the Wide Field Sensor of the Indian Remote Sensing satellite.

71
00:03:31,925 --> 00:03:33,426
And in northwest India,

72
00:03:33,426 --> 00:03:36,427
they made a map of the palaeochannels buried under the ground.

73
00:03:36,427 --> 00:03:39,054
A palaeochannel is the path of an old river

74
00:03:39,054 --> 00:03:40,930
that has now been buried.

75
00:03:40,930 --> 00:03:43,696
The resolution of this sensor was 188 metres.

76
00:03:43,696 --> 00:03:46,463
Resolution means how big the smallest part of the picture

77
00:03:46,463 --> 00:03:48,439
is on the ground.

78
00:03:48,439 --> 00:03:51,996
It means anything smaller than 188 metres cannot be seen separately.

79
00:03:51,996 --> 00:03:56,343
But to see the long paths of a river, this was enough.

80
00:03:56,343 --> 00:03:59,900
And in the images, long, wide lines stood out clearly.

81
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:02,421
But just looking from the sky is not enough.

82
00:04:02,421 --> 00:04:04,943
You also have to go to the ground and check.

83
00:04:04,943 --> 00:04:09,266
Even today, a small stream called Sarsuti flows in Haryana.

84
00:04:09,266 --> 00:04:10,346
It is seasonal,

85
00:04:10,346 --> 00:04:14,309
meaning it brings water only in the rainy season. Listen to the name,

86
00:04:14,309 --> 00:04:16,830
Sarsuti, how similar it is to Saraswati.

87
00:04:16,830 --> 00:04:18,038
In 2009,

88
00:04:18,038 --> 00:04:23,673
a study by ISRO scientists was published in the Journal of the Geological Society of India.

89
00:04:23,673 --> 00:04:27,699
In it, satellite images and ground surveys were used together.

90
00:04:27,699 --> 00:04:30,919
The result was that today's Sarsuti stream

91
00:04:30,919 --> 00:04:34,542
connects with that large buried river network.

92
00:04:34,542 --> 00:04:37,360
That same network they called the Vedic Saraswati.

93
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:39,342
Then came the Central Ground Water Board,

94
00:04:39,342 --> 00:04:42,316
which keeps track of the country's groundwater.

95
00:04:42,316 --> 00:04:44,298
They did drilling along these paths,

96
00:04:44,298 --> 00:04:46,611
meaning they made deep holes in the ground to test it.

97
00:04:46,611 --> 00:04:50,245
Deep below, they found reserves of fresh water. These are called aquifers,

98
00:04:50,245 --> 00:04:54,210
meaning layers of sand and rock inside the earth filled with water.

99
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:58,021
And the most interesting thing. Along the banks of this old river,

100
00:04:58,021 --> 00:05:00,688
the ruins of many Harappan settlements are found.

101
00:05:00,688 --> 00:05:04,880
Harappan means the Indus Valley Civilization, India's oldest city-based civilization.

102
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:07,929
People always settle near a river.

103
00:05:07,929 --> 00:05:11,740
So these ruins tell us there was once plenty of water here.

104
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:16,073
So the picture so far is something like this. The scriptures say,

105
00:05:16,073 --> 00:05:18,831
Saraswati was between the Yamuna and the Sutlej.

106
00:05:18,831 --> 00:05:21,982
The satellite shows the path of the buried river there.

107
00:05:21,982 --> 00:05:24,345
Drilling shows fresh water below.

108
00:05:24,345 --> 00:05:28,679
And old settlements are found on the banks. It seems the story is over.

109
00:05:28,679 --> 00:05:31,830
But the real debate starts right here.

110
00:05:31,830 --> 00:05:35,683
In science, a single piece of evidence is not enough. That there was an old river here,

111
00:05:35,683 --> 00:05:37,434
most people accept this.

112
00:05:37,434 --> 00:05:40,936
But was this the same Saraswati of the Rigveda? And if it was,

113
00:05:40,936 --> 00:05:45,489
was it really a great river flowing from the mountains to the sea?

114
00:05:45,489 --> 00:05:47,240
Here the experts are divided.

115
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:50,914
The first debate is about the source of the water. Some scientists say,

116
00:05:50,914 --> 00:05:54,255
this river received water from Himalayan glaciers.

117
00:05:54,255 --> 00:05:56,927
A glacier means a river of ice frozen on the mountains,

118
00:05:56,927 --> 00:05:59,599
which melts slowly. Others say,

119
00:05:59,599 --> 00:06:01,938
in the Bronze Age,

120
00:06:01,938 --> 00:06:04,610
it was filled only by monsoon rain.

121
00:06:04,610 --> 00:06:08,049
Understand the difference. A river fed by a glacier keeps flowing even in summer,

122
00:06:08,049 --> 00:06:11,831
and doesn't dry up easily. A rain-fed river fills up in the monsoon,

123
00:06:11,831 --> 00:06:14,582
and shrinks in the other seasons.

124
00:06:14,582 --> 00:06:15,958
So the question became:

125
00:06:15,958 --> 00:06:19,397
What kind of river did the Harappan people live beside?

126
00:06:19,397 --> 00:06:21,460
One research study gave an answer.

127
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:25,254
This research was published in a scientific journal called Nature Communications.

128
00:06:25,254 --> 00:06:27,530
It used geomorphology,

129
00:06:27,530 --> 00:06:30,565
meaning reading the shape of the land to understand its history.

130
00:06:30,565 --> 00:06:34,359
Dating was also done, meaning how old the sand is,

131
00:06:34,359 --> 00:06:35,876
worked out through science.

132
00:06:35,876 --> 00:06:39,670
And they found out when the Sutlej moved away from this route.

133
00:06:39,670 --> 00:06:43,646
The result was surprising. By around 8,000 years ago,

134
00:06:43,646 --> 00:06:46,429
the Sutlej had already moved away from the Ghaggar's course.

135
00:06:46,429 --> 00:06:50,803
That is, long before the Harappan cities were settled. Meaning when the cities were built,

136
00:06:50,803 --> 00:06:54,382
the big Sutlej was not flowing along this route.

137
00:06:54,382 --> 00:06:57,960
The cities were settled along its old, abandoned course.

138
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:00,092
The second debate is about a name. The Ghaggar-Hakra,

139
00:07:00,092 --> 00:07:04,000
this dry river that runs from Haryana toward Rajasthan.

140
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,487
Is this the river the Rigveda called Saraswati?

141
00:07:06,487 --> 00:07:09,685
Historian Romila Thapar says this identification is disputed,

142
00:07:09,685 --> 00:07:11,817
meaning it cannot be taken as certain.

143
00:07:11,817 --> 00:07:15,370
According to her, many questions on this are still open.

144
00:07:15,370 --> 00:07:19,484
Scientist and writer Rajesh Kochhar says something different.

145
00:07:19,484 --> 00:07:23,141
In his book The Vedic People, he argues

146
00:07:23,141 --> 00:07:27,255
that the early Saraswati may have been the Helmand river of Afghanistan.

147
00:07:27,255 --> 00:07:32,283
The old name of that region was Haraxvati. Haraxvati and Saraswati,

148
00:07:32,283 --> 00:07:35,940
the two words sound quite similar.

149
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:38,776
In this thinking, there is a term, toponym,

150
00:07:38,776 --> 00:07:41,258
meaning the name of a place or a river.

151
00:07:41,258 --> 00:07:44,804
According to Kochhar's argument, when people moved ahead,

152
00:07:44,804 --> 00:07:46,931
they carried the names along with them.

153
00:07:46,931 --> 00:07:51,541
Like how people today name a new neighbourhood after their old one.

154
00:07:51,541 --> 00:07:54,023
On the other side, scientists in the ISRO study

155
00:07:54,023 --> 00:07:56,150
link the Ghaggar-Hakra itself to the Saraswati.

156
00:07:56,150 --> 00:07:58,586
So what is known, and what is not?

157
00:07:58,586 --> 00:08:01,719
It is known that there are old river channels here.

158
00:08:01,719 --> 00:08:04,155
It is known that there is fresh water underground.

159
00:08:04,155 --> 00:08:06,939
But exactly when and why this river dried up,

160
00:08:06,939 --> 00:08:09,724
nobody has a firm timeline for that yet.

161
00:08:09,724 --> 00:08:12,160
A few big questions are still open.

162
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:13,213
Let's start with tectonics.

163
00:08:13,213 --> 00:08:17,075
Tectonics means the slow shifting of the plates under the ground.

164
00:08:17,075 --> 00:08:19,532
When the land rises and sinks a little,

165
00:08:19,532 --> 00:08:21,989
rivers change their course.

166
00:08:21,989 --> 00:08:26,904
Did some such movement turn this river's water somewhere else?

167
00:08:26,904 --> 00:08:29,010
The exact timing is not certain yet.

168
00:08:29,010 --> 00:08:30,816
Then comes the question of rivers changing direction.

169
00:08:30,816 --> 00:08:33,225
Research has come out on the Sutlej moving away.

170
00:08:33,225 --> 00:08:35,633
But when and how the Yamuna's course changed,

171
00:08:35,633 --> 00:08:37,740
that is not completely clear yet. Just think,

172
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:41,052
if the Sutlej and the Yamuna both once brought water this way,

173
00:08:41,052 --> 00:08:43,460
then this must really have been a big river.

174
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:45,771
And lastly, the question of climate.

175
00:08:45,771 --> 00:08:50,779
Aridification means an area slowly becoming dry and desert-like.

176
00:08:50,779 --> 00:08:54,631
When the monsoon weakened, the rain-fed rivers kept getting thinner.

177
00:08:54,631 --> 00:08:58,483
Was this the real reason this river dried up?

178
00:08:58,483 --> 00:09:01,950
Or did tectonics and climate together cause it?

179
00:09:01,950 --> 00:09:03,436
Now the most practical part.

180
00:09:03,436 --> 00:09:07,523
If you want to see this story yourself, where would you go?

181
00:09:07,523 --> 00:09:11,982
The good news is that its traces are still on the ground today.

182
00:09:11,982 --> 00:09:14,954
You just have to keep your eyes open carefully. Come,

183
00:09:14,954 --> 00:09:16,440
let's walk along the map.

184
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:18,973
Let's walk along the Sarsuti stream in Haryana.

185
00:09:18,973 --> 00:09:22,592
After the monsoon you can see water in it, and sand in summer.

186
00:09:22,592 --> 00:09:26,211
Village people still link it with the Saraswati today.

187
00:09:26,211 --> 00:09:27,658
This is the very stream

188
00:09:27,658 --> 00:09:31,639
that the ISRO study connected to the old buried network.

189
00:09:31,639 --> 00:09:33,810
A small stream, but a big story.

190
00:09:33,810 --> 00:09:37,156
Then towards Rajasthan, look at the wide, dry Ghaggar bed.

191
00:09:37,156 --> 00:09:40,837
Paat means the bed of the river where water once flowed.

192
00:09:40,837 --> 00:09:42,844
In many places it is so wide

193
00:09:42,844 --> 00:09:44,852
that it doesn't look like a small river's.

194
00:09:44,852 --> 00:09:47,529
Nearby you find mounds of Harappan settlements,

195
00:09:47,529 --> 00:09:50,540
that is, high heaps of earth where digging has been done.

196
00:09:50,540 --> 00:09:53,916
And now a place of faith, the Triveni Sangam in Prayagraj.

197
00:09:53,916 --> 00:09:58,042
Here the Ganga and Yamuna meet, you can see it with your own eyes.

198
00:09:58,042 --> 00:10:01,418
Folk tradition says the Saraswati also meets here,

199
00:10:01,418 --> 00:10:05,169
but underground, in an invisible form. This is a legend,

200
00:10:05,169 --> 00:10:07,044
it has no written source,

201
00:10:07,044 --> 00:10:09,670
But many people have deep faith in it.

202
00:10:09,670 --> 00:10:13,793
Please note, this folk tale is connected to the story of the Mahabharata.

203
00:10:13,793 --> 00:10:17,541
The scriptures say the Saraswati went underground at Vinasana.

204
00:10:17,541 --> 00:10:21,664
Folk tradition says that, flowing underground, she reached Prayagraj.

205
00:10:21,664 --> 00:10:25,412
We have no scientific proof of this yet.

206
00:10:25,412 --> 00:10:29,160
But standing at the Sangam, people feel her presence.

207
00:10:29,160 --> 00:10:31,368
So keep the whole story together.

208
00:10:31,368 --> 00:10:33,945
The scriptures speak of a mighty river.

209
00:10:33,945 --> 00:10:37,993
Satellites and drilling show a buried old river. Research says,

210
00:10:37,993 --> 00:10:40,937
the large Sutlej had moved away before the cities.

211
00:10:40,937 --> 00:10:43,882
And scholars differ on the name. There is evidence,

212
00:10:43,882 --> 00:10:46,090
but the final answer is still pending.

213
00:10:46,090 --> 00:10:50,033
Next time we will go toward the sea, to the Gujarat coast.

214
00:10:50,033 --> 00:10:53,976
There, under the water, another story lies buried, that of Dwarka.

215
00:10:53,976 --> 00:10:56,485
What the scriptures say, what the divers found,

216
00:10:56,485 --> 00:11:00,069
and what experts believe. Everything, with evidence,

217
00:11:00,069 --> 00:11:02,220
and a label on every claim.

218
00:11:02,220 --> 00:11:03,407
Now a question for you.

219
00:11:03,407 --> 00:11:07,364
In your opinion, where did the Saraswati's water come from,

220
00:11:07,364 --> 00:11:10,924
from the Himalayan glaciers or from monsoon rain?

221
00:11:10,924 --> 00:11:13,694
Just write in the comments, glacier or rain.

222
00:11:13,694 --> 00:11:17,651
And if there is a dried-up river near your village,

223
00:11:17,651 --> 00:11:19,233
tell us its name too.
